**Failure Mode Analysis**

| Failure Mode | Detection | Recovery / Fallback |
|---|---|---|
| Control rod stuck (cannot withdraw) | Actuator current spike; position sensor no change | Redundant actuator (2×); manual backup (if EVA possible). Otherwise, reactor remains subcritical—safe, but power unavailable (mission partial failure). |
| Control rod stuck (cannot insert/throttle) | Power output not responding to command; temperature rising | Excess power is rejected through additional passive heat pipes to the radiators; reactor inherently self-regulates via negative temp coefficient. Shutdown is not required for safety—only for maintenance or mission end. |
| Heat pipe failure (single pipe) | Temperature gradient increase in core (asymmetry) | Redundant heat pipe design (24 pipes, only 6 required for full power). Loss of 1–2 pipes: core temperature redistributes; power output unchanged. |
| Stirling converter failure (one unit) | Voltage drop from one converter | Redundant converters: 6 for 10 kW, with 2 spares for a 10 kW system; converter can be bypassed with zero loss. |
| Radiator degradation (emissivity drop) | Temperature rise of Stirling cold end (converter efficiency drops) | Deploy a backup radiator boom (folded, stored on the lander); or increase power throttling to 70% load until next mission. |
| Lunar dust accumulation on radiator | Emissivity drop; temperature rise | EDS (electrodynamic screen) on radiator—same technology as Topic 131; or robotic brush cleaning. |
