**Final Verdict**

**【Breakthrough Level】**

*Reasoning:* Kilopower is the only existing technology that can provide > 1 kW continuous power on the lunar surface for multi-year missions without resupply. The KRUSTY test (2018) was the first real space reactor test in over 50 years, successfully demonstrating startup, steady-state operation, and throttling > 16:1 at 800°C, with all objectives met or exceeded . The 10 kW system mass (1,500 kg) is landable on a single commercial lander; 4 units provide 40 kW—sufficient for ISRU propellant production and habitat life support for a 4–6 person crew . Compared to PV + battery (50+ tons for 100 kW) or RTG (impossible at 10 kW due to Pu-238 shortage), Kilopower is the **only viable path** to sustained lunar outposts. This is not a linear improvement—it is a **paradigm shift** from sunlight-dependent, battery-buffered power to continuous, nuclear-grade reliability.

**Preemptive Q&A**

**Q: 93% enriched uranium—isn't that weapons-grade material, with all the non-proliferation and security concerns?**

A: Yes, HEU is regulated. The Kilopower project uses HEU because it allows a small, compact core that is launchable cold. The DOE NNSA manages the fuel stockpile and has established protocols for space reactor fuel—Kilopower is a US government program (NASA + DOE), and fuel is drawn from existing national inventory . For international or commercial applications, the reactor would require different enrichment levels (e.g., > 20% but < 93%—HALEU) or an alternative fuel cycle; however, for the initial lunar base, the US government can deploy this under existing authority.

**Q: The Stirling converters have moving parts—won't they wear out over 10 years?**

A: Free-piston Stirling converters have only two moving parts (the piston and the displacer), both with gas bearings—no mechanical contact, no lubrication required. NASA has demonstrated > 100,000 hours of continuous operation (11+ years) in ground tests. The KRUSTY test operated for 28 hours; ground validation extrapolates to 10+ years with < 5% degradation. In the 10 kW system, the Stirling converters are modular and can be replaced robotically if needed (spares are carried with the lander) .

**Q: What happens if the radiator is shadowed by regolith dust or the reactor overheats?**

A: The reactor has a self-regulating negative temperature coefficient: higher temperature → core thermal expansion → reduced neutron density → fission rate drops. This is passive physics, not active control. Even with total loss of the radiator (impossible—the radiator is oversized and redundant), the reactor would heat to a new equilibrium at a slightly higher temperature, not a runaway. The KRUSTY testing demonstrated a transient of < 15°C under "loss of coolant" conditions, well below the 50°C requirement .

**Q: Can Kilopower be used for nuclear electric propulsion (NEP), not just surface power?**

A: Yes. Kilopower is multi-mission capable . For NEP, 5–10 kW units can be clustered or scaled up to 50 kW. The KRUSTY core was tested at 5 kW thermal and could have produced 20 kW if testing allowed; scaling to 50 kW (e.g., for a cryobot mission to Europa's ocean) is under study . However, the primary NASA application is surface power (Moon/Mars), not propulsion—the same reactor architecture can support both.

**Q: How do you cool the reactor during launch (it's launched cold, but still has decay heat from U-235's natural alpha decay)?**

A: U-235 has a half-life of 700 million years—its natural decay heat is negligible (a few watts). The reactor is launched cold (subcritical) with the control rod fully inserted. There is no fission heat; the only heat source is natural alpha decay, which is < 10 W and passively radiated from the core without any active cooling. This is why Kilopower can be launched on any standard rocket without special nuclear safety provisions beyond the standard non-proliferation and emergency response protocols .

